  "answer": "**Short answer: don't order comp1 based on the risk score — the warning can't support that decision. Order (or not) based on stock cover instead.**\n\n**Risk (from `get_failure_risk`, machine 30, as_of 2015-10-14):** comp1 scores 0.262, but that probability is not established as better than the base rate on held-out data (`calibrated: false`), so treat it as a ranking signal, not a likelihood. Its `warning_adequacy` is **insufficient** — meaning even if the score were reliable, the detection lead time (~24 hours) is far shorter than either comp1 part's supplier lead time (10 and 17 days). A warning that arrives a day before failure cannot be acted on with a 10–17 day lead time part. So this number should not drive an order.\n\n**Parts position (from `get_parts_position`, comp1):**\n- PN-COMP1-001 (model1): 22 in stock, ~41.7 units/30d consumption → **15.8 days of cover**, 10-day lead time.\n- PN-COMP1-002 (models 2–4): 32 in stock, same consumption rate → **23.0 days of cover**, 17-day lead time.\n\nCover exceeds lead time for both parts, so on stock/consumption grounds alone there's no urgent reorder trigger right now for either comp1 part — but PN-COMP1-001's margin (15.8 days cover vs 10-day lead time) is tighter and worth watching if consumption rises.\n\n**What I can't tell you:** which specific machine/part will fail, or when within any 14-day window; and I can't validate this against real plant behavior — this is trained on the Microsoft Azure PdM simulation only.",
